1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,482
You have seen the videos. A robot with two arms and two

2
00:00:03,532 --> 00:00:07,014
legs folds a towel, badly, then well. Another one walks across a

3
00:00:07,064 --> 00:00:10,546
warehouse carrying a box. Another sits by a bed in a care

4
00:00:10,596 --> 00:00:14,078
home in Japan and purrs when you stroke it. Every one of

5
00:00:14,128 --> 00:00:17,610
those videos ends the same way, with the same question in the

6
00:00:17,660 --> 00:00:21,142
comments: who is in charge of this thing? I spent the last

7
00:00:21,192 --> 00:00:24,674
year trying to answer that question with data instead of opinions. Not

8
00:00:24,724 --> 00:00:28,206
who should be in charge. Who is. Tonight I want to show

9
00:00:28,256 --> 00:00:31,738
you what the rules of the world actually say about machines that

10
00:00:31,788 --> 00:00:35,270
think and move. The short version is: less than you think, about

11
00:00:35,320 --> 00:00:38,802
different robots than you think, and the gap is in the one

12
00:00:38,852 --> 00:00:42,334
place we are about to put them.

13
00:00:43,384 --> 00:00:47,174
Somewhere, right now, a robot is being built to live in your

14
00:00:47,224 --> 00:00:51,014
mother's room. Not a factory arm behind a fence with a yellow

15
00:00:51,064 --> 00:00:54,854
line on the floor. A machine that lifts her when she cannot

16
00:00:54,904 --> 00:00:58,694
lift herself. That carries things. That reminds her to take the pills.

17
00:00:58,744 --> 00:01:02,534
That watches her at night, and talks back when she talks to

18
00:01:02,584 --> 00:01:06,374
it. Every company in this field is building toward that room, because

19
00:01:06,424 --> 00:01:10,214
that is where the money is and, honestly, because that is where

20
00:01:10,264 --> 00:01:14,054
the need is. So here is the question I could not stop

21
00:01:14,104 --> 00:01:17,894
asking. When that machine is in that room, what rules will it

22
00:01:17,944 --> 00:01:21,734
be following? Whose rules? Written when, and by whom?

23
00:01:22,784 --> 00:01:27,159
So we went looking for the rules. All of them. Laws, regulations,

24
00:01:27,209 --> 00:01:31,583
standards, government strategies, scientific papers, news. Three thousand three hundred and twenty

25
00:01:31,633 --> 00:01:36,008
documents, from a hundred and nineteen countries, the European Union and the

26
00:01:36,058 --> 00:01:40,433
United Nations system. And I have to tell you something about how

27
00:01:40,483 --> 00:01:44,858
we read them, because it matters. No human read them all. Nobody

28
00:01:44,908 --> 00:01:49,282
could. An AI did. It asked every single document the same thirty-nine

29
00:01:49,332 --> 00:01:53,707
questions. Is this binding? What machine is it about? What does it

30
00:01:53,757 --> 00:01:58,132
say about safety, about who is in control, about who pays? Then

31
00:01:58,182 --> 00:02:02,557
other AIs checked its answers, and an independent one audited them. I

32
00:02:02,607 --> 00:02:06,981
will come back to how much you should trust that. Keep it

33
00:02:07,031 --> 00:02:11,406
in mind. For now, here is what the map shows.

34
00:02:12,456 --> 00:02:16,544
Two hundred and seventy-eight. That is how many binding rules there are

35
00:02:16,594 --> 00:02:20,681
on Earth that reach a robot. Binding means laws with teeth: break

36
00:02:20,731 --> 00:02:24,819
them and something happens to you. A fine, a ban, a prison

37
00:02:24,869 --> 00:02:28,956
sentence. We counted each one once, with its amendments but without its

38
00:02:29,006 --> 00:02:33,094
paperwork, because legal databases will hand you the same law as twelve

39
00:02:33,144 --> 00:02:37,232
separate entries if you let them. Two hundred and seventy-eight. For every

40
00:02:37,282 --> 00:02:41,369
robot, every drone, every self-driving car, every machine that moves on its

41
00:02:41,419 --> 00:02:45,507
own, in every country we could reach. That number is a floor;

42
00:02:45,557 --> 00:02:49,644
we certainly missed some. But it is not a thousand, and it

43
00:02:49,694 --> 00:02:53,782
is not ten thousand.

44
00:02:54,832 --> 00:02:59,736
And of those two hundred and seventy-eight, twenty-five mention artificial intelligence. Twenty-five.

45
00:02:59,786 --> 00:03:04,689
The other two hundred and fifty-three were written for something else. For

46
00:03:04,739 --> 00:03:09,643
aircraft. For vehicles. For machinery. For consumer products. For medical devices. They

47
00:03:09,693 --> 00:03:14,596
reach robots by accident of definition. Robots, it turns out, are governed

48
00:03:14,646 --> 00:03:19,550
sideways.

49
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:25,166
Here is what sideways looks like. A hundred and forty-six of the

50
00:03:25,216 --> 00:03:29,782
rules are about drones. Why? Because a drone is, legally, an aircraft,

51
00:03:29,832 --> 00:03:34,398
and we have had aviation law for a century. A hundred and

52
00:03:34,448 --> 00:03:39,014
nine are about self-driving cars. Because a self-driving car is, legally, a

53
00:03:39,064 --> 00:03:43,630
vehicle, and every country has a road-traffic act. Thirteen are about factory

54
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:48,246
robot arms. Because an arm is, legally, a machine, and machinery law

55
00:03:48,296 --> 00:03:52,862
has been protecting workers since the steam engine. None of those laws

56
00:03:52,912 --> 00:03:57,478
began with someone asking, what should a thinking machine be allowed to

57
00:03:57,528 --> 00:04:02,094
do? They began with a category. The robot fell into it.

58
00:04:03,144 --> 00:04:07,724
So what about the robots that do not fall into an old

59
00:04:07,774 --> 00:04:12,353
category? The robot in your mother's room. The companion. The humanoid. We

60
00:04:12,403 --> 00:04:16,983
counted. Binding rules for companion robots: zero. For humanoids: zero. For care

61
00:04:17,033 --> 00:04:21,612
robots: four, and all four are laws about something else, a robotics

62
00:04:21,662 --> 00:04:26,242
industry act here, a special economic zone there, that mention care robots

63
00:04:26,292 --> 00:04:30,872
in passing. Four hundred and twenty-nine documents in our collection are about

64
00:04:30,922 --> 00:04:35,501
these three kinds of machine. Four of them have legal force. The

65
00:04:35,551 --> 00:04:40,131
one text in the world written specifically for care robots is a

66
00:04:40,181 --> 00:04:44,760
voluntary engineering standard from 2014. That is the entire legal inheritance of

67
00:04:44,810 --> 00:04:49,390
the machine that will help your mother out of bed.

68
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:54,614
Now here is the part that surprised me most. We also had

69
00:04:54,664 --> 00:04:58,838
five hundred and nine scientific papers in the collection, and we asked

70
00:04:58,888 --> 00:05:03,062
which robots they were about. Forty-three percent of the research on robot

71
00:05:03,112 --> 00:05:07,286
governance is about companion and social robots. Three percent of the rules

72
00:05:07,336 --> 00:05:11,510
are. Scientists are studying the robots we will live with: how people

73
00:05:11,560 --> 00:05:15,734
bond with them, whether they deceive, what happens when a lonely person

74
00:05:15,784 --> 00:05:19,958
depends on one. Lawmakers are regulating the ones already in the street.

75
00:05:20,008 --> 00:05:24,182
The two communities are looking at different machines. We checked whether that

76
00:05:24,232 --> 00:05:28,406
was an artefact of where we searched. It is not. The gap

77
00:05:28,456 --> 00:05:32,630
is real, and it is widest exactly where the machines are heading.

78
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:38,386
Why does it happen this way? Because rules follow machines into public

79
00:05:38,436 --> 00:05:43,143
space, and usually they follow the first accident. Look at the pattern.

80
00:05:43,193 --> 00:05:47,899
Drone rules came mostly between 2016 and 2021, after the near-misses at

81
00:05:47,949 --> 00:05:52,655
airports, after the first drone hit a passenger aircraft's wing. Self-driving rules

82
00:05:52,705 --> 00:05:57,412
came from 2017 on, after the test fleets were already on the

83
00:05:57,462 --> 00:06:02,168
road, and after the first deaths. Sidewalk delivery robot laws came between

84
00:06:02,218 --> 00:06:06,925
2018 and 2022, after the robots were already on the pavement, bumping

85
00:06:06,975 --> 00:06:11,681
into people's ankles. Three quarters of all these rules were written in

86
00:06:11,731 --> 00:06:16,437
the last seven years. We regulate what has already hurt somebody. The

87
00:06:16,487 --> 00:06:21,194
robots in the videos have not hurt anybody yet. That is why

88
00:06:21,244 --> 00:06:25,950
they have no rules. And that is the window.

89
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,923
Let me tell you why this is not just the AI debate

90
00:06:30,973 --> 00:06:34,896
with a different hat on. When software goes wrong you get a

91
00:06:34,946 --> 00:06:38,869
bad sentence or a bad score. When a machine with a body

92
00:06:38,919 --> 00:06:42,842
goes wrong you get an event. And so the questions change. Can

93
00:06:42,892 --> 00:06:46,815
it hurt you? A two-hundred-kilogram arm can. How close may it come?

94
00:06:46,865 --> 00:06:50,788
Who pays when it breaks something, or someone? And the hardest one:

95
00:06:50,838 --> 00:06:54,760
how much may it decide by itself before a person has to

96
00:06:54,810 --> 00:06:58,733
step in? We counted what the rules actually spell out. Physical safety,

97
00:06:58,783 --> 00:07:02,706
in detail, in a hundred and sixty-seven of the two hundred and

98
00:07:02,756 --> 00:07:06,679
seventy-eight. Fairness, in three. Human dignity, in one. The AI ethics conversation

99
00:07:06,729 --> 00:07:10,652
of the last decade, bias, transparency, dignity, is almost absent from the

100
00:07:10,702 --> 00:07:14,625
rulebook for machines with bodies. The rulebook for a body is about

101
00:07:14,675 --> 00:07:18,598
the body.

102
00:07:19,648 --> 00:07:23,835
And that hardest question, how much may it decide alone, is where

103
00:07:23,885 --> 00:07:28,073
the rules go quiet. A hundred and seventy-one of them raise it.

104
00:07:28,123 --> 00:07:32,310
They say there shall be appropriate human control. Seventy-three actually answer it:

105
00:07:32,360 --> 00:07:36,547
what is allowed, by whom, under what conditions, checked how. And almost

106
00:07:36,597 --> 00:07:40,785
all of those seventy-three are in aviation and road law, where remote

107
00:07:40,835 --> 00:07:45,022
pilot and fallback driver already mean something. Everywhere else, the law gestures

108
00:07:45,072 --> 00:07:49,259
and moves on. Mention is not detail. And the distance between mention

109
00:07:49,309 --> 00:07:53,497
and detail is where the lawsuits of the next ten years are

110
00:07:53,547 --> 00:07:57,734
going to live.

111
00:07:58,784 --> 00:08:02,838
One more thing we asked every rule: who, in your own words,

112
00:08:02,888 --> 00:08:06,942
has to obey you? Sixty-two percent name the companies that operate the

113
00:08:06,992 --> 00:08:11,046
machine. Thirty-nine percent name the companies that build it. Eighteen percent name

114
00:08:11,096 --> 00:08:15,150
you, the person in the room with it. And eight percent, one

115
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:19,254
rule in twelve, name the people who wrote the software that does

116
00:08:19,304 --> 00:08:23,358
the deciding. The duty lands on whoever runs the machine in the

117
00:08:23,408 --> 00:08:27,462
world. The people who shape its judgement are almost nowhere in the

118
00:08:27,512 --> 00:08:31,566
law. If that strikes you as backwards, you are not alone.

119
00:08:32,616 --> 00:08:36,783
Now the promise I made. We used AI to read the laws

120
00:08:36,833 --> 00:08:41,000
about AI, and you should want to know how much to trust

121
00:08:41,050 --> 00:08:45,217
that. So we did something simple. We took a random hundred of

122
00:08:45,267 --> 00:08:49,435
those binding rules and gave them to a separate AI auditor with

123
00:08:49,485 --> 00:08:53,652
three strict tests: is this really a distinct law, is it really

124
00:08:53,702 --> 00:08:57,869
binding, does it really say something about a machine? Seventy-three passed. A

125
00:08:57,919 --> 00:09:02,086
year ago it was fifty-four. Seventy-three is good. Seventy-three is not a

126
00:09:02,136 --> 00:09:06,303
hundred. What failed is instructive: laws that mention a drone in a

127
00:09:06,353 --> 00:09:10,520
list of an agency's duties, index pages captured instead of the statute,

128
00:09:10,570 --> 00:09:14,737
the same European regulation counted twice. And the number of these documents

129
00:09:14,787 --> 00:09:18,955
a human has labelled, so far, is zero. This is a map

130
00:09:19,005 --> 00:09:23,172
drawn by machines, waiting for a person to walk it. Every number

131
00:09:23,222 --> 00:09:27,389
I have given you tonight is an honest estimate with an honest

132
00:09:27,439 --> 00:09:31,606
error bar. Please check them. The whole thing is public.

133
00:09:32,656 --> 00:09:37,371
So what do we do with the window? Three things, before the

134
00:09:37,421 --> 00:09:42,136
robots arrive. One. Write the rules first this time. Companions, carers, humanoids:

135
00:09:42,186 --> 00:09:46,901
zero, four, zero. We can see them coming. We know what room

136
00:09:46,951 --> 00:09:51,666
they are headed for. Drones did not get that courtesy; they got

137
00:09:51,716 --> 00:09:56,431
their rules after the airport. Two. Answer the control question; do not

138
00:09:56,481 --> 00:10:01,197
just raise it. Who may let the machine decide what, under which

139
00:10:01,247 --> 00:10:05,962
conditions, checked by whom. Aviation did it. Everyone else can. Three. Make

140
00:10:06,012 --> 00:10:10,727
the safety knowledge public. The documents that say how close, how fast,

141
00:10:10,777 --> 00:10:15,492
how much force, are engineering standards, and fifty-seven of the sixty-one we

142
00:10:15,542 --> 00:10:20,257
found are behind a paywall. We could not read them, and neither

143
00:10:20,307 --> 00:10:25,022
can you. Rules you cannot read are rules you cannot follow.

144
00:10:26,072 --> 00:10:29,975
I started with the videos, and the question in the comments. Who

145
00:10:30,025 --> 00:10:33,928
is in charge of this thing? Here is the honest answer, from

146
00:10:33,978 --> 00:10:37,880
three thousand documents. For the machines in the street and the sky,

147
00:10:37,930 --> 00:10:41,833
a patchwork of old law, stretched. For the machines that are about

148
00:10:41,883 --> 00:10:45,786
to walk into our homes, almost nobody. Not yet. The question is

149
00:10:45,836 --> 00:10:49,739
not whether robots will come into our homes. They are coming. The

150
00:10:49,789 --> 00:10:53,692
strategies are written, the factories are tooling up, the demonstrations are on

151
00:10:53,742 --> 00:10:57,644
every feed. The question is whether the rules will be there when

152
00:10:57,694 --> 00:11:01,597
they do. Right now, the answer is no. And for once, we

153
00:11:01,647 --> 00:11:05,550
can see it coming. Thank you.

